      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hey, I'm trying to set up some reorder logic for our electronics parts. We're seeing some issues with lithium-ion battery cell stockouts when we hit peak season. Can you help me draft a formula or some basic rules for calculating safety stock levels, specifically considering lead times from our Shenzhen suppliers?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Safety stock, you're looking at, is a crucial buffer against unexpected demand spikes or supplier delays. A common approach is to calculate it based on the desired service level, the variability of demand, and the variability of lead time. You can use a formula like: $\\text{Safety Stock} = Z \\times \\sigma_{LT} \\times \\sqrt{\\text{Avg Lead Time}}$. Think of $Z$ as the Z-score corresponding to your service level (e.g., 1.645 for 95% service).\n\nFor your Shenzhen suppliers, since lead times can be unpredictable, you should track the historical variance of those delivery times. If your demand is highly volatile during peak season, you might need to increase your safety stock multiplier significantly. I'd suggest starting by calculating the standard deviation of demand during the lead time period. This will give you a solid, data-driven starting point.",
      "source": "target",
